Great Wall, sometimes specifically referred to as the CfA2 Great Wall, is an immense galaxy filament. It is one of the largest known superstructures in the observable universe.","CfA2_Great_Wall",null,"Margaret Geller",null,"Discovery of CfA2 Great Wall",{"id":"evt-1t8rf6x-cizy0z","sourceIdentity":"discovery|CfA2_Great_Wall|CfA2 Great Wall|1989|38.9|-77.04","mediaKey":"CfA2_Great_Wall|1989|38.9|-77.04","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CfA2_Great_Wall"},"kind":"point","start":{"iso":null,"precision":"year","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CfA2_Great_Wall"}},"end":null}],["Discovery of 52-hertz whale",0,-160,1989,1989,"sci",3,"Pacific Ocean","The 52-hertz whale, colloquially referred to as 52 Blue, is an individual whale of unidentified species that calls at the unusual frequency of 52 hertz in the north Pacific Ocean between Aleutian and Kodiak Islands to the California coast. The whale itself has never been sighted: it has only been heard via hydrophones, but its call has been detected since the late 1980s in a pattern that matches the migration of the blue whale and the fin whale. Those species call…","52-hertz_whale",null,"Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution",null,"Discovery of 52-hertz whale",{"id":"evt-g66h3-kk0385","sourceIdentity":"discovery|52-hertz_whale|52-hertz whale|1989|0|-160","mediaKey":"52-hertz_whale|1989|0|-160","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/52-hertz_whale"},"kind":"point","start":{"iso":null,"precision":"year","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/52-hertz_whale"}},"end":null}],["Discovery of Laguna Copperplate Inscription",14.3,121.46,1989,1989,"sci",3,"Lumban","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is an official acquittance certificate inscribed onto a copper plate in the Shaka year 822. It is the earliest-known, extant, calendar-dated document found within the Philippines.","Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription",null,null,null,"Discovery of Laguna Copperplate Inscription",{"id":"evt-637ats-1bx087i","sourceIdentity":"discovery|Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription|Laguna Copperplate Inscription|1989|14.3|121.46","mediaKey":"Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription|1989|14.3|121.46","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription"},"kind":"point","start":{"iso":null,"precision":"year","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription"}},"end":null}],["Discovery of skip list",39,-76.93,1989,1989,"sci",3,"University of Maryland","In computer science, a skip list is a probabilistic data structure that allows  average complexity for search as well as  average complexity for insertion within an ordered sequence of  elements. Thus it can get the best features of a sorted array while maintaining a linked list-like structure that allows insertion, which is not possible with a static array. Fast search is made possible by maintaining a linked hierarchy of subsequences, with each successive subsequence skipping over fewer…","Skip_list",null,"William Pugh",null,"Discovery of skip list",{"id":"evt-1huu1ih-i3jje9","sourceIdentity":"discovery|Skip_list|skip list|1989|39|-76.93","mediaKey":"Skip_list|1989|39|-76.93","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skip_list"},"kind":"point","start":{"iso":null,"precision":"year","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skip_list"}},"end":null}],["Discovery of Fukuisaurus",35,136,1989,1989,"sci",3,"Japan","Fukuisaurus is a genus of herbivorous ornithopod dinosaur that lived during the Early Cretaceous in what is now Japan. The type species is F. tetoriensis, which was named and described in 2003.","Fukuisaurus",null,null,null,"Discovery of Fukuisaurus",{"id":"evt-123nc9k-16ldzbu","sourceIdentity":"discovery|Fukuisaurus|Fukuisaurus|1989|35|136","mediaKey":"Fukuisaurus|1989|35|136","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuisaurus"},"kind":"point","start":{"iso":null,"precision":"year","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuisaurus"}},"end":null}],["D-brane introduced",30.3,-97.73,1989,1989,"sci",3,"University of Texas at Austin","In string theory, D-branes, short for Dirichlet membrane, are a class of extended objects upon which open strings can end with Dirichlet boundary conditions, after which they are named. \nD-branes are typically classified by their spatial dimension, which is indicated by a number written after the D. A D0-brane is a single point, a D1-brane is a line, a D2-brane is a plane, and a D25-brane fills the highest-dimensional space considered in bosonic string theory. There are also instantonic D(−1)-branes…","D-brane",null,"Joseph Polchinski",null,"D-brane introduced",{"id":"evt-1tboi2b-1lew303","sourceIdentity":"discovery|D-brane|D-brane|1989|30.3|-97.73","mediaKey":"D-brane|1989|30.3|-97.73","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D-brane"},"kind":"point","start":{"iso":null,"precision":"year","source":{"type":"discovery","file":"events_wikidata.json","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D-brane"}},"end":null}],["Discovery of Ohalo",32.72,35.57,1989,1989,"sci",3,"","Ohalo II is an archaeological site in the Northern District, Israel, near Kinneret, on the southwest shore of the Sea of Galilee. It is one of the best preserved hunter-gatherer archaeological sites of the Last Glacial Maximum, radiocarbon dated to around 23,000 BP (calibrated). It is at the junction of the Upper Paleolithic and the Epipaleolithic, and has been attributed to both periods. The site is signi